WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.230
- Were you aware at the time that,

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:02.730
in addition to making fake bombs,

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.880
the FBI had been recruiting
Mr. Fayed for 18 months?

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:06.713
- No, ma'am.

00:00:06.713 --> 00:00:07.630
- That he has no history of violence,

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:09.620
no history of terrorist affiliation?

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:10.460
- He doesn't know that.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:11.994
- Has no evidence indicating he's--

00:00:11.994 --> 00:00:12.827
- Object!

00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:13.660
- Any interest in terrorism--
- Objection!

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.820
- Prior to being recruited
by this team of agents?

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:17.653
- No, ma'am.

00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:21.753
(light music)

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:26.200
- Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.700
Today we're covering a
show called For the People.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.600
I don't know anything about this show,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.830
but it was highly
requested by you out there,

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.570
so keep those recommendations coming.

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.960
I'm always looking for new portrayals of

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.270
lawyers and courtrooms in the media.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.420
As always, be sure to comment

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.510
in the form of an objection,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.780
which I will either sustain or overrule.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:44.770
And while you're there, let me know

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.110
what movie or TV show I should do next.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.150
And of course, stick around
until the end of this video,

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.200
where I give the first
episode of For the People

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.890
a grade for legal realism.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.900
So, without further ado, let's dig into

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.071
the first episode of For the People.

00:01:01.071 --> 00:01:02.240
(jazzy music)

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.333
All right, New York.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.500
It's always New York for some reason.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.450
Ah, the famous New York Supreme Court.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.560
Oh, but that's a different courthouse.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.870
Oh, that is the Riverside,
California courthouse.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.200
I know that courthouse very well,

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.440
both the New York one
and the California one.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.450
- There are two great courts in America,

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.563
the Supreme Court and the one
you're sitting in right now.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.440
This is the United States District Court

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:37.310
for the Southern District of New York,

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:40.390
the oldest, most
prestigious, highest profile

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.330
trial court in America.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:46.340
- I don't know if the SCNY
is the oldest US court.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:47.590
I would think that it was created

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.070
at the same time as the
other district courts,

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:50.560
but there's no doubt that the

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.950
Southern District of New York

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.900
is one of the most high profile

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.870
federal courts in the United States.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.870
There is a ton of high profile litigation

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.030
that goes on in the Southern
District of New York

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.810
because that is where New York City is,

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.220
so there are a lot of
high profile cases there.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.250
So, it is not an exaggeration to say that

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.450
it is one of the most prestigious places

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:09.840
to be an assistant US attorney

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.250
or a federal public defender.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.680
- This is the court that heard claims

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.630
over the sinking of the Titanic,

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.140
this is the court that heard the case

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:18.780
against the Rosenbergs.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.963
Aaron Burr was a lawyer in this court.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.460
- You wanna claim Aaron Burr?

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.950
- The cases are hard, stakes are high,

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.690
the lawyers on the other
side better than you,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:35.120
and the judges are smarter than you.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.760
Some of you won't succeed.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.450
Some of you are not worthy.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.900
But for those of you who are,

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.163
your time here will be the
highlight of your career.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.800
- All right, that's not a crazy speech

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.170
to be given by a judge at
a swearing in ceremony.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:57.520
That's, I can imagine that
actually happening in real life.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.000
- [All] So help me God.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.663
- Welcome to the mother court.

00:03:01.663 --> 00:03:03.860
(dramatic music)

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.630
- And they're walking
out of the Supreme Court,

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:07.880
the State Court, I think.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:09.840
That's funny.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.740
So, interestingly, this
takes place in federal court,

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.210
not state court the way
that Law and Order does.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.250
Law and Order takes place
entirely within the state court.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.650
But the federal courts
exist for two reasons.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.910
Number one, there are federal laws.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:25.220
As you may know, there
are two types of law

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:26.880
in the United States,
there are those that are

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.780
created by the state governments,

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.000
and there are the laws that are

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.070
created by the federal government.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.430
Sometimes there's overlap,
sometimes there isn't.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:34.890
But if you have a case that deals

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:38.350
specifically with federal
law and federal jurisdiction,

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.890
you're going to be litigating your case

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.550
in the federal courts.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.900
And then additionally, especially if

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.130
you're on the civil side,

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.110
you'll deal with what's
called diversity jurisdiction,

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.020
where if you have defendants who are

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.670
citizens of different states,

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.000
the federal courts were designed

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.780
to handle litigation
amongst different states

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.360
and amongst citizens of different states

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.700
to make sure that everyone
gets a fair shake.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:59.580
But this looks like it's
gonna be a criminal show

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.940
'cause we're dealing with
the federal prosecutors

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:03.930
and the federal public defenders,

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:05.360
so that leads me to believe that this show

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.470
is probably dealing exclusively with

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.123
federal criminal law,
as opposed to civil law.

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.070
- Good morning everyone, I'm Jill Carlon.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:14.700
I'm the Federal Public Defender.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.610
Inside these envelopes is your first case.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:20.860
And let me warn you, you're not gonna win.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.810
You're up against the
United States government,

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:24.350
and the government almost always wins

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:26.003
because they have all the power.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:28.700
- Some truth to that.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:29.970
So, the advice that this

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.070
head public defender is giving them,

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:35.380
while a little cynical, is
probably pretty accurate.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:36.340
You're probably not gonna take

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:37.480
a lot of these things to trial,

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.070
you're probably not gonna go to the mat

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:41.460
on a lot of these cases,
but you're going to plead,

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.470
you're gonna give these people

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.470
the best defense you possibly can,

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:47.523
even if that results in
guilty pleas down the line.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:50.470
- What's duty?

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.220
- The attorney on duty picks
up any cases that come in.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:54.550
- Do you need someone today?

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:56.450
- Yes, but you have to
have some experience

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:57.490
to be on duty, Sandra.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:58.330
- I have experience.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.670
- You have experience
clerking on the Supreme Court,

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:03.140
which is great experience
for being a justice

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:03.973
on the Supreme Court.

00:05:03.973 --> 00:05:05.800
It doesn't have anything
to do with this job.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:07.429
I'll put you on duty when you're ready.

00:05:07.429 --> 00:05:08.280
- I'm ready.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.113
- Yes, that is accurate.

00:05:09.113 --> 00:05:10.500
If you clerk for the Supreme Court,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.780
that is the most prestigious thing

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.790
you could possibly do, it's
a huge star on your record,

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:15.570
but it doesn't mean you necessarily have

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.530
on the ground experience,
although some people

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:20.070
do have experience before
they clerk the Supreme Court.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:23.360
But it would not be a
Shonda Rhimes legal TV show

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.410
if there wasn't someone that
clerked for the Supreme Court.

00:05:26.410 --> 00:05:28.880
It shows up in all of her
shows for some reason.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.030
- Why would I give you his case?

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.857
- Because a farm animal
could win this case,

00:05:31.857 --> 00:05:33.140
and that case actually matters,

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.020
and if it matters, you
should give it to me.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.620
When I was clerking on the Supreme Court--

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.190
- Another Supreme Court clerk, wow.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:41.023
- Is that true?

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:43.760
Leonard doesn't think
you're ready for this case.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.490
- Well, he's right, neither one of us

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:46.620
is ready for a case like this,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.430
but I'm at least humble
enough to recognize it,

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:50.680
and I'll work that much
harder because of it.

00:05:52.244 --> 00:05:54.827
(quirky music)

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:01.930
I've never been a fan of humility.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.230
- That's so ridiculous.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.280
Also, where are their
supervising attorneys?

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:08.870
You're not gonna handle a terrorism case

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.600
on your first day as an
AUSA, that's ridiculous.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:12.980
- Do a good job on your new case

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:15.210
and you'll get recognized
because you're good,

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:18.030
and decent, and you do
things the right way,

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:20.470
which is what matters
in the long run, okay?

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.080
Okay?

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.160
Now, I have to go because I have

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.250
my first appearance as an attorney

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.920
in the Southern District of New York.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:29.220
- First appearance?

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:30.720
It's your first day!

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:31.720
- What's the charge?

00:06:33.202 --> 00:06:34.770
- 18 USC 2332(a)(3).

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:35.840
You picked this up on duty?

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:36.990
- Yes.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.300
- That can't be on duty, 2332(a)(3) is--

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.870
- Attempted use of a
weapon of mass destruction

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:42.090
against any property that is owned,

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.420
leased, or used by the United States

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:46.790
or any department or agency
of the United States, I know.

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:47.900
- No, you don't.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.630
That's ridiculous that anyone would have

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:53.530
encyclopedic knowledge
of the entire US Code

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:54.990
just memorized like that.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.720
The entire US Code is bigger than

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:58.270
all of those books put together,

00:06:58.270 --> 00:06:59.820
and it is ridiculous to expect that

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.570
anyone would have the
code section memorized,

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:04.910
even just the criminal
law sections memorized.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:06.720
It's preposterous.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.450
- Your Honor, as alleged in the complaint,

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.750
Madeline McCarna was an
administrative assistant

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:13.610
at Ilaria Pharmaceuticals,
and illegally traded on

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:15.850
inside information regarding a merger

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:17.380
between Ilaria and Biogen.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.070
- Excuse me, Your Honor,
but the complaint actually

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:20.460
does not allege that.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:22.760
It alleges that Ms. McCarna
passed on information

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.020
to her ex-husband, who
executed the trades,

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.360
facts which we deny.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:27.367
- [Judge] What was the
value of the trades?

00:07:27.367 --> 00:07:30.957
- $9325 and 54 cents, Your Honor.

00:07:30.957 --> 00:07:32.173
- $9000?

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.280
This is what Roger Dun is prioritizing

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.130
in the US Attorney's Office nowadays?

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:43.290
- So, unlike most shows I've seen,

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.270
they were actually arguing with the judge

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.100
behind council table, which is generally

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:48.470
where you're gonna be arguing.

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.120
You're not gonna be
wandering inside of the well

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.830
like you see on TV, so
that kind of quality

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:56.710
is probably something you
would see in real life,

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:58.604
and a judge is likely to get upset about

00:07:58.604 --> 00:08:01.700
a matter that is taking
up the government's time

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:03.330
over just $9000.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.180
That's a very small
case, so I would expect

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:07.780
the judge to be annoyed by that.

00:08:08.874 --> 00:08:10.350
- If they're offering a deal like that

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.830
in a case like this, there must be

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:12.960
something wrong with the evidence.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.790
- Maybe, but if you wanna find out,

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:16.110
you're gonna have to reject the offer,

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:17.140
and if you reject the offer,

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:18.708
you're going to trial--

00:08:18.708 --> 00:08:20.150
- Well, maybe.
- You're going to lose.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:22.130
- Well, also maybe.

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:23.210
- I can give it to someone else.

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:24.620
- No.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.560
- Just because you reject a plea deal

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:28.190
early on in the case doesn't mean that

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:30.730
that plea deal will no longer be available

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:32.590
throughout the rest of the case.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:34.650
This particular case
is probably gonna take

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:37.340
a year or two before
it even gets to trial.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.940
There'll be months, if
not years of litigation,

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:43.300
and it would not be insane to
reject the first plea deal,

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:44.720
especially when you haven't even

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.300
talked to your client yet.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:48.190
So, she's not really in a position to know

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:50.500
whether the plea deal is
good or bad at this point,

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.870
so of course, if there's a choice,

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:54.280
she's going to have to reject it,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:56.610
and by the way, she's gonna
have to talk to her client

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.410
about whether to accept or
reject the plea deal too.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.320
So, I understand the cynicism
from the head PD here

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.727
that you're just trying to
plead these things down,

00:09:04.727 --> 00:09:07.230
but you can't do that on your first day

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.720
when you don't know any of
the facts in this situation.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:11.553
- Are you okay?

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:13.893
- I'm gonna do this.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:20.340
- You know how we've always said--

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.640
- That is a really nice
apartment in New York.

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:26.340
- You rejected a plea deal
without talking to me.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.390
- You said talk to my client,
I talked to my client.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.890
- Well, you didn't talk to
me, and that was a mistake,

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:33.110
and you can't afford to
make mistakes like that

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:33.990
on a job like this.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:35.790
- Yeah, you really need to talk to

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:37.483
your supervising attorney there.

00:09:37.483 --> 00:09:40.080
That's a big deal right there.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.500
- Well, Kate, here's the problem, for you:

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:44.140
my client, and let's keep it simple

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.740
and call him Adam Dial, couldn't have

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.110
committed the alleged fraud because,

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:50.520
as you probably have tabbed there

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:51.650
in one of your binders,

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:55.474
fraud requires an intent to deceive--

00:09:55.474 --> 00:09:57.660
- Oh, no, not the mens rea argument.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.100
- Because he actually
believes all this is real.

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:00.933
Crazy?

00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:05.860
Possibly, but the upshot
is, no intent, no crime.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:06.790
- He is technically right.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:08.540
I assume that the crime of fraud

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:09.780
is probably similar to the similar to

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.630
the civil requirements for fraud,

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:14.010
which do require an intent to deceive,

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:17.800
or a knowing falsity of the
representations that one makes.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:19.590
That being said, that is a very, very

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:20.760
difficult argument to make,

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:22.730
especially given that there will be

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:23.910
other circumstantial evidence,

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:26.480
like the fact that he
took all of the money

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.430
from the people that he defrauded

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:30.160
and used it for his own purposes.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.290
You don't have to necessarily
bring a psychologist in.

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:34.850
You can just say that,
well, he took the money

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:36.570
from these people that he defrauded,

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:38.710
and therefore, that shows that he had

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:39.810
the required mental state,

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.820
and that's probably
going to carry the day.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:44.073
Good luck in court, man.

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:48.570
- Yeah, he's buying the whole thing.

00:10:49.541 --> 00:10:50.950
Yeah, everything, that's what I'm saying.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.484
The Bureau, the technology upgrade.

00:10:53.484 --> 00:10:55.990
Yeah, I've gone full primal fear on him.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:58.250
Yeah, this guy's a real tool.

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:00.240
- Tab four of my discovery guide,

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.943
request jailhouse recordings.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.900
- Nice, that is 100% accurate.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:07.610
When you make a phone call in jail,

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:09.130
unless it's to your lawyer,

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.050
that is definitely discoverable.

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:13.300
In fact, most jails
have a warning that says

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.720
your phone call will be recorded,

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.210
so you don't have a reasonable
expectation of privacy,

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:21.220
and you should be on notice
that your phone calls

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.330
are going to be recorded, and
they can be used against you.

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:28.170
This is a little bit dramatic,
but definitely could happen.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:29.740
- I just don't understand why we're

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:31.245
prosecuting a case like this

00:11:31.245 --> 00:11:35.430
against a secretary for a $9000 trade.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.710
- Well, that is a problem that
you don't understand that,

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:40.210
so let me spell it out for you:

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:42.447
you're prosecuting the
case 'cause I told you to,

00:11:42.447 --> 00:11:44.060
and I told you to prosecute the case

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:45.780
'cause that secretary broke the law.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:47.000
Every now and then, regular people--

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:47.833
- Fair enough.

00:11:47.833 --> 00:11:48.666
- Need to be reminded that there are

00:11:48.666 --> 00:11:51.470
serious consequences to breaking
the law, whatever the law--

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:52.540
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:53.950
- Stop signs.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:55.540
People start doing whatever
the hell they feel like,

00:11:55.540 --> 00:11:58.060
our whole system breaks
down and cars crash,

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:00.480
markets crash, people get hurt.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.330
- Yeah, and it'll piss off the judge,

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:03.940
but he's absolutely right.

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:05.980
A crime is a crime, and just
because it's a small amount

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:08.790
doesn't mean that it
shouldn't go unpunished.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.690
- What, what is this?

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:12.680
- Extra papers to fit in your bag.

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:14.460
You should always carry a lot of paper.

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:16.698
They get worried you have
something they don't know about.

00:12:16.698 --> 00:12:18.010
(laughing)

00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:19.020
- That's clever.

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:21.540
- This case is about attempt
and you can't prove attempt,

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.930
because he was arrested before

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.280
he got to the Statue of Liberty.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:25.850
- Attempt only requires
the defendants take

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:26.683
a substantial step--
- Yep.

00:12:26.683 --> 00:12:27.893
- Toward the commission of a crime.

00:12:27.893 --> 00:12:28.841
- Very true.

00:12:28.841 --> 00:12:29.674
- He was on Liberty Island with a bomb.

00:12:29.674 --> 00:12:30.690
- Then why was the plan always to wait

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:32.090
to arrest him until he got to the crown,

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:35.495
because that's what you thought
you needed to prove attempt?

00:12:35.495 --> 00:12:37.630
- They're young, they're
hungry, they're smart.

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:38.910
Let them fight it out.

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:40.210
- Now, the prosecutors are right.

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:41.740
You can have an attempted crime

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.350
with really just minimal actions

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:47.310
taken toward the final
act of the crime itself.

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:49.659
So, taking what he thought was a bomb

00:12:49.659 --> 00:12:53.219
to Liberty Island would
absolutely qualify as

00:12:53.219 --> 00:12:55.283
attempted terrorism here.

00:12:57.723 --> 00:12:58.672
- You know you're part of

00:12:58.672 --> 00:13:01.098
an important tradition in my life?

00:13:01.098 --> 00:13:02.220
I'm going to trial tomorrow.

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:04.180
- Going to trial tomorrow, what?

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:06.363
Have years passed since his first day?

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:08.303
- Are you nervous?

00:13:08.303 --> 00:13:09.590
- Is it that spicy?

00:13:09.590 --> 00:13:12.006
- The trial, are you
nervous about the trial?

00:13:12.006 --> 00:13:13.173
- No, I'm not.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:17.900
I'm gonna win.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:18.870
- How many trials have you done

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:20.810
if you clerked for the Supreme Court?

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:22.220
Probably zero.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:24.890
And where is his team
of other lawyers too?

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:26.620
You're not gonna go to
trial with just one person

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:29.563
on a terrorism case in the
Southern District of New York.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:33.030
- Please, tell the jury
what happened that morning.

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:36.200
- Well, I saw this young
man come of the ferry

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:37.630
carrying a camera.

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:39.840
He was sweating, jittery.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:42.080
I asked to see his camera,
and that's when I realized

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.140
it was a bomb.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:46.520
- A fake bomb made by the FBI.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:47.910
- I didn't know that at the time.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:48.990
- Were you aware at the time that,

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.420
in addition to making fake bombs,

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:53.590
the FBI had been recruiting
Mr. Fayed for 18 months?

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:54.700
- No, ma'am.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.620
- She can't really ask those
questions of this witness.

00:13:56.620 --> 00:13:59.020
This witness is a percipient
witness to what happened

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:01.480
on the day that the
defendant was arrested,

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:03.070
so all of the stuff that happened

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:06.310
before he got there is totally
irrelevant for this witness.

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:07.690
He has no personal knowledge of that

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:09.720
and he can't really opine or testify

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:11.010
about those percipient facts.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.830
This is the wrong witness
to ask those questions.

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:14.870
- Were you aware at the time that,

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:16.370
in addition to making fake bombs,

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:19.610
the FBI had been recruiting
Mr. Fayed for 18 months?

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:20.443
- No, ma'am.

00:14:20.443 --> 00:14:21.280
- That he has no history of violence,

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:23.024
no history of terrorist affiliation?

00:14:23.024 --> 00:14:24.110
- He doesn't know that.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:25.816
- Has no evidence indicating he's ever had

00:14:25.816 --> 00:14:28.050
any interest in terrorism prior to being

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:30.460
recruited by this team of agents?

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:31.293
- No, ma'am.

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.180
- You remember Mr. Fayed well, sir?

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:36.020
- [Witness] Yes.

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:36.900
- Hmm, really?

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:38.556
How long have you worked on that ferry?

00:14:38.556 --> 00:14:39.389
- 19 years.

00:14:39.389 --> 00:14:41.814
- 19 years, and how many ferries a day?

00:14:41.814 --> 00:14:43.060
- [Witness] 25, 26.

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:45.470
- Thousands of ferries, tens
of thousands of visitors,

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:46.820
and you want the jury to believe that

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:48.000
you remember my client?

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.130
Not just remember him, remember him well?

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:50.963
- Yes.

00:14:50.963 --> 00:14:51.930
- Well, unless your memory has been

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:53.530
jogged by the government, Mr. Rockton,

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:55.890
how could you possibly
remember my client at all?

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:59.130
- Because I quit my job
because of him, that's how!

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:00.680
Because that ferry he was on

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:03.210
was the last ferry I ever worked.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:07.000
I was down there on 9/11, ma'am,

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.730
on the island, right next to her.

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:13.323
The idea that someone would
wanna blow her up now?

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:16.903
I remember.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:19.860
- Don't ask a question you don't know

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:20.990
the answer to on cross.

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:22.846
- [Jill] Never ask a question
you don't know the answer to.

00:15:22.846 --> 00:15:23.800
(laughing)

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:24.633
- Nice.

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:30.650
Okay, closing argument.

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:32.430
- We live in a dangerous time.

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:33.680
- I'm gonna assume he got permission

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.580
to enter the well to talk to the jury.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:40.140
- Our enemies used to be overseas,

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:41.420
and we could see them.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.290
Tanks and airplanes.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:45.105
They wore uniforms.

00:15:45.105 --> 00:15:47.770
- Oh, that is not a proper argument here.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:51.210
- But now we're living on the battlefield.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.080
- I mean, he's relying on things

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.150
that are outside of this trial.

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:56.300
You gotta be really careful

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:57.650
when you make this kind of argument

00:15:57.650 --> 00:15:59.830
and this kind of appeal to emotion.

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:03.500
I don't think a prosecutor's
gonna get away with that.

00:16:03.500 --> 00:16:06.370
- They look like us, they talk like us,

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:07.670
they live down the hallway from us.

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:08.980
We only find out who they are

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:11.920
and the damage they wanna
do after they've done it.

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:12.753
- Oh, man.

00:16:12.753 --> 00:16:13.620
- After they've shot up a nightclub--

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:14.453
- That's not proper.

00:16:14.453 --> 00:16:15.360
- Or blowing up a marathon.

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:16.710
- Oh, come on, man!

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:20.460
You can't bring in facts
from other terrorist acts

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:21.970
as your closing argument for this

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:24.020
particular criminal trial here.

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:26.700
That is not proper at all.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.920
This is a big gaff here.

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:30.110
- Ms. Bell wants you to believe that

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:31.970
our government did this.

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:33.060
Ask yourself how much money,

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.990
how much time would it
take to convince you

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.190
to blow the head off
the Statue of Liberty.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:42.453
- He's making some
contradictory arguments here.

00:16:43.390 --> 00:16:46.160
- That man is not a criminal,

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:48.400
and law enforcement
prevented no crime here.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:50.210
They manufactured a crime.

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:52.270
Instead of hunting actual terrorists,

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:55.380
your government tried to make a terrorist.

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:59.070
They contacted him, they befriended him.

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:01.120
- It's really unrealistic that these

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:04.040
first year attorneys
are going to be making

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:05.010
the closing arguments here.

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:06.920
This is a high profile case.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:09.850
It would almost certainly be their bosses

00:17:09.850 --> 00:17:12.110
or their bosses' bosses that
are making these arguments

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:14.330
and handling this trial, not these

00:17:14.330 --> 00:17:15.880
really, really young attorneys,

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.380
regardless of the fact if they

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:18.630
clerked for the Supreme Court.

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:19.983
That just won't apply.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:22.570
- They picked a target for him,

00:17:22.570 --> 00:17:26.070
they built a bomb for him, they made him.

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:27.810
- Yeah, but he took the act.

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:28.920
It's a totally separate act.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:30.910
I don't think that defense applies here.

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.585
I think he has taken the predicate acts.

00:17:33.585 --> 00:17:36.160
Regardless of whether
the bomb worked or not,

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:39.210
he thought it did, so he
had the right mens rea.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:41.410
I don't think the
entrapment defense works.

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:42.790
I don't think they really manufactured

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:43.960
his particular acts.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:45.370
They may have helped him along,

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:46.700
but I don't think that rises to

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:48.603
the defense of entrapment here.

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:53.730
- It's easy to become
distracted in a case like this,

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:56.543
to become emotional, to
focus on the wrong things.

00:17:57.397 --> 00:18:00.980
But this case is about one
thing and one thing only:

00:18:00.980 --> 00:18:04.130
it is about the power of
the United States government

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:08.110
being used to systematically
target, and manipulate,

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.450
and incarcerate American citizens

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:13.020
because of their religion!

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:14.530
- I mean, there's been
no evidence of that.

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:16.623
- Their national origin, their color.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:20.060
- I mean, she's bringing
in outside facts too.

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.850
- But what you have today
is even more powerful.

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:26.283
You have the power to say no.

00:18:27.269 --> 00:18:28.750
You have the power to resist.

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:31.220
In this courthouse, in this courtroom,

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:35.103
in those seats today, you have
the awesome power of justice.

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:40.393
And there's nothing
more powerful than that.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:46.750
- Yeah, I think she's gonna lose.

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:49.100
- Versus Mohammed Fayed--
- I think he's guilty.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.940
- We the jury find the
defendant, Mohammed Fayed,

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:54.408
guilty of one count of attempted use of--

00:18:54.408 --> 00:18:56.260
- Called it.

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:58.583
- You uh, do you mind if I sit here?

00:19:05.123 --> 00:19:07.706
(tragic music)

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:12.763
- Jesus, so morose.

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:15.360
You just won a big trial.

00:19:16.690 --> 00:19:18.340
- I told you you were gonna lose.

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:20.413
I told you.

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:23.386
This isn't TV.

00:19:23.386 --> 00:19:24.728
(laughing)

00:19:24.728 --> 00:19:25.673
- Okay.

00:19:28.077 --> 00:19:29.253
- [Sandra] What did I get?

00:19:29.253 --> 00:19:30.086
- What?

00:19:30.086 --> 00:19:31.840
- You said on the first
day, get something.

00:19:32.850 --> 00:19:34.023
What did I get today?

00:19:35.210 --> 00:19:36.303
- You got beat.

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:39.693
And now you gotta get up.

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:48.040
- And also, you'll be able
to appeal and probably win

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:50.690
and cause a new trial
because the prosecutor

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:53.050
made ridiculous arguments
in his closing argument,

00:19:53.050 --> 00:19:55.350
so there's always that,
there's always appeal.

00:19:57.180 --> 00:19:59.240
- There it is, right there.

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:00.570
See it?

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:01.433
The mother court.

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:04.573
- Are we worthy?

00:20:06.290 --> 00:20:09.790
- Yes, I think so, we are.
- We are worthy.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:13.141
- You just handled your
own trial as first chair.

00:20:13.141 --> 00:20:14.279
- We are so worthy.

00:20:14.279 --> 00:20:15.112
- Who is worthier than us?

00:20:15.112 --> 00:20:16.223
- Aaron Burr?
- Please!

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:18.928
(bang)

00:20:18.928 --> 00:20:21.511
(quirky music)

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:25.590
- Okay, now it's time
to give For the People

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:28.093
a grade for legal realism.

00:20:28.093 --> 00:20:30.100
(knocking)

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:32.380
It was on the cusp of being the most

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:33.520
accurate show I've ever seen,

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:35.100
but didn't quite make it.

00:20:35.100 --> 00:20:38.020
It was overly dramatic,
you had things happening

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:40.750
on a timescale that
isn't really believable.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:42.670
But for the most part, they are making

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:44.210
semi-reasonable arguments,

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:45.520
the things that are happening

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:47.600
could actually happen in real life.

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:50.060
It is far more accurate than
I ever thought it would be.

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:52.810
So, at the end of the
day, I'm going to give

00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:56.650
For the People episode one an A minus,

00:20:56.650 --> 00:21:00.380
a shockingly accurate legal
procedural from Shonda Rhimes.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:01.300
I can't believe it.

00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:02.880
You know, a lot of people become lawyers

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:05.820
because they think they're
bad at math and science.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:07.120
If only they'd learned the right way,

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:09.410
there would be fewer lawyers in the world.

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:11.430
That's why Brilliant.org created the best

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:14.130
interactive app for learning
how to problem solve.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:16.210
From basic logic to high school algebra

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:17.850
to computer science fundamentals,

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.100
Brilliant turned learning into a game.

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:22.190
If only Brilliant had been
around when I was a student,

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:23.420
I might not have gone into law

00:21:23.420 --> 00:21:25.330
and actually done something
good for the world.

00:21:25.330 --> 00:21:26.740
Whether you're a student who wants to get

00:21:26.740 --> 00:21:28.110
better math grades, or a parent

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:30.010
who wants their kid to be a scientist,

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:32.090
Brilliant can help you learn how.

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:35.440
Get better at STEM and actually
prevent future lawyers.

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:37.940
If you visit Brilliant.org/LegalEagle

00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:39.620
or click the link in the description,

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:41.270
you can sign up for free and learn

00:21:41.270 --> 00:21:44.660
all kinds of things not
related to being a lawyer.

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:46.270
And as a bonus for Legal Eagles,

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:47.910
the first 200 people will also get

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:50.270
20% off their annual membership.

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:53.740
Remember, only you can
stop future lawyers,

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:56.883
and until next time,
I'll see you in court.

